("earbuds-buying-checklist", "buying", "guide",
"The earbuds buying checklist: what actually matters before the price tag",
 "Fit, isolation, codecs, battery honesty and the call-mic test - the seven checks that decide whether earbuds earn their keep, in the order to check them.",
"""<p>Earbuds are the most-bought and least-researched gadget in tech, which is why so many drawers hold a pair that almost worked. The marketing sells features; the daily experience is decided by seven unglamorous checks. None of them is the brand name.</p>
<h2>The seven checks, in order</h2>
<p><b>1. Fit and isolation come before everything.</b> Two identical earbuds can sound opposite on two ears - a bad seal eats bass first, so the "weak sound" complaint is usually a tip-size problem. Multiple tip sizes in the box, or a fit style you have worn happily before: that is check one, and it outranks everything below. <b>2. Battery honesty.</b> The headline number is the case; the earbuds alone matter more - four to six hours per charge is the honest modern range, and anything under four makes long days into socket-hunting. Check the claimed hours for the buds, not the case. <b>3. Codecs: baseline is enough.</b> SBC and AAC (the defaults on Android and iPhone respectively) carry the overwhelming majority of real listening; LDAC and aptX are nice on paper and audible on fewer occasions than the spec sheet implies. Do not pay a premium for a codec your phone cannot use - the same logic as <a href="/tech/phone-buying-specs-that-matter/">buying phones by the specs that matter</a>.</p>
<p><b>4. Noise cancelling: know what you are buying.</b> Active noise cancelling hushes steady droning - planes, trains, fans - and does remarkably little against voices and sudden sounds. For commutes it is genuine; for an open office it is partial. Passive isolation from a good fit does half the work for free. <b>5. The call microphone.</b> Music you hear is half the product; your voice on calls is the other half and almost never demoed. Look for reviews that test calls in wind and street noise - this is where cheap buds fail first. <b>6. Latency, if you watch video.</b> Sound lagging the picture is the classic budget-bud failure; any modern pair with a low-latency mode or Bluetooth 5-era chip is fine. <b>7. IP rating, if they will sweat.</b> The rating means exactly what it says and nothing more: IPX4 handles rain and workouts; nothing consumer-grade is for swimming.</p>
<h2>Then, and only then, the price</h2>
<p>Decide your three non-negotiables from the seven, ignore the rest of the sheet, and buy the cheapest pair that clears your three - the price bands move every season, but the checklist does not. The same discipline scales to bigger purchases: <a href="/tech/laptop-buying-ram-storage/">laptops by RAM and storage</a>, <a href="/tech/monitor-buying-specs/">monitors by the specs that matter</a>, and the honest middle path of <a href="/tech/refurbished-vs-new-tech/">refurbished versus new</a>.</p>""",
 [],
 [("phone-buying-specs-that-matter", "Phone specs that matter"), ("refurbished-vs-new-tech", "Refurbished vs new"), ("laptop-buying-ram-storage", "Laptops by RAM and storage")]),
